  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing Disaster Preparedness and capacity for initial rapid response - NorthK</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In the Horn of Africa, East and Central Africa (HECA) region, more than 47 million people were already food insecure due to the triple crisis of floods, locust invasion and now COVID -19.  According to the Integrated Food Security Phase Classification (IPC)  - November 2021, the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) is experiencing one of the worst food crises in the world, with 27 million people, or 26% of the population, experiencing high acute food insecurity. The impact of conflict, the primary cause of food insecurity, is long -lasting.  This project will help reduce the impact of humanitarian disasters by more than 8 million people in phase 3+ of acute food insecurity identified in eastern DRC; with a likely increase of 0.21% by the end of the first half of 2022. The strengthening of basic services and other infrastructure (water, hygiene and sanitation, health and education services within the community) must continue to ensure dignified living conditions and equitable access for all communities. Host areas Internally Displaced Persons (PDIs) are often subject to risks and protection incidents due to cohabitation and promiscuity within host families; support for the process of reconciliation between host communities (displacement or return) will be necessary. Mechanisms for prevention and mitigation of protection risks will need to be put in place. Host communities also need access to information on available humanitarian assistance and communication channels need to be adapted to their preferences. In order to prevent tensions and conflict over land between communities. Support for the restoration and development of basic social services and/or essential infrastructure and economic recovery will be considered for returnees. In particular, it is important to ensure the protection and respect of basic rights of people affected by the crisis, especially for women heads of households with regard to their access to land, and to prevent them from being discriminated aga</narrative>
  </description>
